          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %em Gauss-Manin connection
            is a canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat connection
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles"} fiber bundles
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+cohomology"} relative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary
            (but possibly
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted
            = succeed "-)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} co
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+groups"} homology groups
            %em parameter
            varies.
          %h2#details Details
          %p (…)
          %h2#ForFiberBundles For fiber bundles
          %p
            The existence of a flat connection on bundles of fiberwise-cohomology groups is easy to understand in the case that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibers"} fibers
            form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+trivial+bundle"} locally trivial
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            %a{:href => "#Voisin02"} Voisin 2002, above Def. 9.13
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Voisin-DefinitionOfGaussManinConn.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
            ), see also
            %a{:href => "#EtingofFrenkelKirillov98"} Etingof, Frenkel &amp; Kirillov 1998, §7.5
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/EtingofEtAl-DefOfGaussManin.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
            ).
          %p
            Simple as this may appear, at least in the case of fiberwise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            this case subsumes (for forgetful fibrations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+spaces+of+points"} configuration spaces of points
            in the plane) the profound example of solutions to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov+equation"} Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov equation
            %a{:href => "Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov+equation#BraidRepresentationsViaTwisteddRCohomologyOfConfigurationSpaces"} hypergeometric integral construction
            – this is highlighted as such in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#EtingofFrenkelKirillov98"} Etingof, Frenkel &amp; Kirillov 1998, §7.5
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExampleOfHypergeometricKZEquations"}
            below.
          %p
            In the following we give (following
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#SS22"} SS22
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretic
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            on a class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles"} fiber bundles
            which subsume the case that gives the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KZ-equation"} KZ-equation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+spaces+of+points"} configuration spaces of points
            (Ex.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ForgetfulMapBetweenConfigurationSpaces"}
            below).
          %p
            This following more abstract argument has the advantage that it applies at once to all
            = succeed "-theories" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            exist (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology+theories"} Whitehead-generalized cohomology theories
            and more general
            = succeed "-theories," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              \-toposes
            and in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %h3#ForNonAbelianCohomology For non-abelian cohomology
          %p
            The following is a (non-standard) general-abstract argument for the existence and construction of the structure of local systems on fiberwise cohomology groups, which applies in the generality of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology+theories"} Whitehead-generalized cohomology theories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology theories
          %p
            The simple idea – using that all these notions of cohomology have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            – is that the fiberwise 0-truncation of the
            %em
              %a{:href => "parameterized+homotopy+theory#FiberwiseMappingSpaces"} fiberwise mapping space
            to that classifying space provides at once the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
            which exhibits the local system.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            We consider the following situation, see also
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:OverviewUntwisted"} (6)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NotationOverview"} (14)
